1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,680
you're paying for AI capability.

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:04,800
But the real skill is knowing when to use

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,020
which level of capability.

4
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:08,240
Claude is not just one model.

5
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:09,720
It gives you a set of models

6
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:11,460
and each one has a different job.

7
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:13,140
Let's look at the model lineup.

8
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:14,200
Start with Sonnet.

9
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:16,660
Claude Sonnet 5 is now the default model

10
00:00:16,660 --> 00:00:17,700
for free and pro users.

11
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:20,300
It is also the model most people should use

12
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:21,260
most of the time.

13
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:22,980
Anthropic describes it

14
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:25,240
as the most agentic Sonnet model yet.

15
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:27,220
That means it is better at planning,

16
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:28,380
using tools,

17
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:30,880
following through on multi-step tasks

18
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,040
and handling everyday knowledge work.

19
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:35,060
Think drafting, summarizing,

20
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:36,120
research synthesis,

21
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:37,380
artifact review,

22
00:00:37,820 --> 00:00:39,720
coding and workflow automation.

23
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:41,940
For most leadership tasks,

24
00:00:42,380 --> 00:00:43,960
Sonnet is the right starting point.

25
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:46,320
It gives you a strong balance of speed,

26
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:47,920
quality and cost.

27
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:50,520
Then there is Haiku, the lightweight model.

28
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:54,020
Use Haiku when speed matters more than depth.

29
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:55,820
It is useful for quick summaries,

30
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:58,400
simple rewrites, classification, routing

31
00:00:58,400 --> 00:00:59,860
or high volume tasks

32
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:01,700
where you do not need deep reasoning.

33
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:03,900
Then there is Opus.

34
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:06,760
Opus 5 is the deeper reasoning model

35
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:09,000
built for complex enterprise work

36
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,020
and long horizon agentic tasks.

37
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:12,820
As Anthropic stated,

38
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:14,360
it is particularly strong

39
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:16,640
at checking and correcting its own work.

40
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:18,520
Use it when the work is harder,

41
00:01:18,940 --> 00:01:20,340
messier or higher stakes.

42
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:23,720
For example, a strategic decision,

43
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:25,600
a complex financial analysis,

44
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:27,540
a sensitive executive message

45
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:29,440
or a multi-step task

46
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:31,100
where the first answer needs to be

47
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:32,400
more complete and considered.

48
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:35,760
Think of it as the specialist you bring in

49
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:38,420
when Sonnet's first pass is not enough.

50
00:01:39,340 --> 00:01:40,740
Start with high effort

51
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:44,000
and only move higher for genuinely demanding work.

52
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:46,580
Lower effort can still produce

53
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:49,000
strong results with less time and usage.

54
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:51,140
Finally, there is Fable.

55
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:54,620
Fable 5 is Anthropic's most powerful model.

56
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:56,600
It was recently released more broadly

57
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:58,440
after being paused for safety updates.

58
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:00,840
It is built for hard thinking tasks

59
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:02,180
and for AI systems

60
00:02:02,180 --> 00:02:05,400
that can plan and carry out many steps over time.

61
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:07,940
For example, it can research a topic,

62
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:10,080
create a plan, write documents,

63
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:11,780
improve them based on feedback

64
00:02:11,780 --> 00:02:14,020
and manage tasks across tools

65
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:15,200
with little human help.

66
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:18,120
However, as the most capable model,

67
00:02:18,620 --> 00:02:20,740
Fable 5 will draw down your usage

68
00:02:20,740 --> 00:02:22,980
much faster than Opus 4.8.

69
00:02:23,340 --> 00:02:25,060
So it is best suited for teams

70
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:27,020
building serious AI systems

71
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:29,660
rather than most daily individual tasks.

72
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:33,400
Now, where do you find and switch these models?

73
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:35,740
To switch models, start a new chat

74
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:37,080
and click the model name

75
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:39,020
in the right corner of the prompt box.

76
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:40,900
That opens the model picker

77
00:02:40,900 --> 00:02:42,800
where you'll see your available options.

78
00:02:43,400 --> 00:02:45,380
You'll also notice the effort options

79
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:46,920
from the model selection dropdown.

80
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:48,760
This lets you decide

81
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:51,200
how much effort Claude should put into a response.

82
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:53,560
Keep in mind, the higher the effort,

83
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:54,560
the slower the response

84
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:56,120
and the faster the credits run out.

85
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:57,420
So how should you choose?

86
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:00,860
Use Haiku when the task is simple and speed matters.

87
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:03,540
Use Sonnet for most daily work,

88
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:06,560
drafting, planning, summarizing, analysis,

89
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:08,520
coding and working with documents.

90
00:03:09,380 --> 00:03:11,980
Use Opus when the task needs deeper judgment

91
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:13,440
or a stronger first pass.

92
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:16,680
Use Fable when you are building with the API,

93
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:18,580
working with very long context

94
00:03:18,580 --> 00:03:20,560
or designing a long running,

95
00:03:20,660 --> 00:03:21,540
agentic workflow.

96
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:24,920
Now, a quick note on availability and pricing.

97
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:28,120
Claude Sonetti 5 is available across all plans

98
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:30,200
and is the default for free in Pro.

99
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:32,860
Claude Opus 428 is available on Pro,

100
00:03:33,300 --> 00:03:34,660
Max, Team and Enterprise.

101
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:37,100
On Pro, usage is capped

102
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:38,980
because Opus costs more compute.

103
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:40,440
If you hit that limit,

104
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:42,000
move to Max for higher usage.

105
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:44,900
Claude Fable 5 is not on the free tier.

106
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:46,940
It is available on paid plans

107
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:49,660
and strictly limited to up to 50%

108
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:51,000
of your weekly usage limits.

109
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:52,340
So keep in mind,

110
00:03:52,500 --> 00:03:54,040
the model you pick shapes the result

111
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:55,440
before you write a single word.

112
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:57,280
Start making that distinction today

113
00:03:57,280 --> 00:03:59,260
and you'll get more out of Claude.
